OROCHIMARU RESEARCH DIVISION
CURSED SEAL PROGRAM — VOLUME II: SURVIVAL AND LONG-TERM EFFECTS
CLEARANCE: RESEARCH CADRE

Abstract
Continuing from Volume I. This volume examines why some bearers survive and thrive while
most perish, and the toll the seal takes on those who live.

Survival Factors
- Chakra reserve at intake remains the dominant predictor. Raise the selection threshold.
- Strength of will governs whether the bearer masters the seal or is mastered by it. This
  cannot be measured at intake and must be observed.
- Emotional volatility raises short-term power but degrades control. Calm bearers stabilize
  faster.

Long-Term Effects
- Sustained use, especially of Level 2, accelerates physical degradation. Bearers must be
  cycled off active duty to recover.
- The seal maintains a standing tie to the caster's influence. Under stress the bearer may
  be drawn toward that influence — a feature, not a fault, but one to manage in the field.
- A properly evolved seal grants a marked leap in output; the strongest bearers rival elite
  operatives.

Recommendation
Retain only high-reserve, strong-willed survivors for advancement to Level 2 trials.
Log all outcomes to the experiment log. Volume III (removal and re-sealing) pending.
